Subject: wasix: create only the host functions a module imports

Instantiating a WASIX module built every function of every WASI namespace
- wasi, wasi_unstable, wasi_snapshot_preview1, wasix_32v1 and wasix_64v1,
several hundred in all - into the new store, whatever the module imported,
then found each of the module's imports in that import object again by
name. Each function is an object the store keeps until it is dropped, so a
spawned process paid to create and then to free every one of them, when a
module imports a few dozen from one or two namespaces.

Each namespace table now answers for a single name as well as for all of
its names: given a name, it creates only the function of that name, if it
has one. wasix_import chooses the table by namespace. WasiEnv::instantiate
resolves the module's imports one by one, in the module's order - the
memory it was given for env.memory, else the WASIX function the import
names, else what the runtime adds - and instantiates the module from that
list. The precedence is the one the import object gave, an import nothing
provides fails to link with the same error, and a runtime import that
duplicates a WASIX one is still skipped with a warning, now when the
module imports it. The dynamic linker still takes an import object, which
holds only the module's WASIX imports. generate_import_object_from_env,
which is given no module, still provides every function of the version
it is asked for. The tables are unchanged apart from each one's signature
and the macro that builds it.

The tests check that every function of every namespace is found by its
name, that a module is instantiated from its imports and runs, that an
import nothing provides fails to link with that error, and that the
linker's import object holds only what the module imports.

